{"text": "a a\n\nPage 77\nX.\u00a2.3. MOLECULAR STUDIES OF NICOTINE REGULATED GENE EXPRESSION\nAND THE CONTROL OF NICOTINIC RECEPTOR GENES.\npriority level (1)\nprincipal Investigator: Evan S. Deneris, PhD :\nco-Investigators: Ss. Younkin, MD, PhD; R. Friedland, MD\nI. RESEARCH PLAN\nSPECIFIC AIMS\n1. Identification of nicotine-regulated genes. The\n\npheochromocytoma PCi2 cell line will be used to a)\nRetermine whether previously identified genes are\nSubject to nicotine regulation and b) identify new\ngenes that are nicotine regulatable-\n2.  egulation of brain nicotinic receptor gene expression.\nTeanseription regulatory elements controlling nicotinic\nreceptor subunit gene expression will be identified\nusing the PCi2 cell line.\n\nBACKGROUND AND SIGNIFICANCE (Short/Long Term Goals)\n\nRecent: epidemiological studies have indicated a possible\nDrotective effect of tobacco-derived nicotine consumption on the\nPreidence of Alzheimer's and Parkinson's disease. This is\npotentially of great clinical importance and thus warrants\npotther investigation. In broad terms, we hypothesize that\nRicotine can influence a brain pathway(s) which in turn results\nTaehe amelioration or prevention of neurodegeneration. Nothing\n{a known about how this might happen, because little is known\nbout how nicotine interacts with the, brain and changes its\napemistry. We do know that nicotine can regulate the expression\nSeon clase of genes, termed immediate early genes; these genes\nGanetion as transcription factors which regulate target genes to\neentrol cellular growth and differentiation.\u2019 This, intriguing\nfiece of information raises the possibility that nicotine can\neiter neuronal gene expression to confer an anti-\nfeurodegenerative phenotype to neurons.\n\nbr. Deneris' laboratory is using molecular techniques to\nQhvestigate the interaction of nicotine with the brain and how\nthe genes encoding nicotine receptors are regulated. This\nspe edreh will contribute to our understanding of how nicotine\nTRtiuences brain chemistry to possibly control the onset and\nprogression of neurodegeneration and the role of nicotine\neeCdbeor gene regulation in neurodegeneration. The short term\ngoal of this research is to determine whether nicotine can\nPguiate the expression previously identified genes that are\nExgressed in the brain. \u2018The long term goal is to identity new\nGenes that are regulated by nicotine, understand how nicotine\nBeegpeor gene expression is controlled and the relevance of each\n\nETPSST6Z02",
